      <span class="stamp dim">REVIEW ARTICLE · DATA VINTAGE {A.vintage}</span>
    </header>

    <article>
      <div class="masthead">
        <span class="eyebrow">METHODS &amp; VALIDATION · FIRE &amp; HAZE EARLY WARNING</span>
        <h1>Can an ignition model beat the Fire Weather Index over Indonesian peat?</h1>
        <p class="standfirst">A stress test of a 0.25° daily ignition-risk surface and a
        back-trajectory attribution engine. The model's skill survives the harder test its own
        design never ran — but the headline metric is the wrong one for a rare event, the
        comparison that gives the case its title rested on a single season until we found the bug
        that stranded the rest, and the attribution, replayed over every episode instead of one,
        points somewhere the page did not.</p>
        <div class="byline">
          <span>Demo Lab · FMV</span><span>Data through {A.vintage}</span>
          <span>{nf(A.published.panel_rows)} cell-days · {nf(A.rescore.n_cells)} land cells</span>
          <span>{nf(TR.receptor_days)} receptor-days</span>
        </div>
      </div>

      <section class="abstract">
        <h2>Abstract</h2>
        <p><b>Question.</b> The case claims an ignition-risk surface with AUC {sig(P1.auc)} at one
        day's lead that "beats the operational Canadian Fire Weather Index", and a 72-hour
        back-trajectory that attributes a bad-air day in Singapore to fires in South Sumatra. We
        ask whether either claim survives an independent re-scoring on the case's own data.</p>
        <p><b>Method.</b> We re-derive every headline statistic from the case's published
        out-of-fold predictions ({nf(A.rescore.sample_rows)} rows across three leads), audit the
        CEMS FWI join row by row, refit the model under a split blocked in <i>space</i> as well as
        season, and replay the trajectory ensemble across all {nf(TR.receptor_days)}
        receptor-days rather than one.</p>
        <p><b>Findings.</b> The published AUC reproduces exactly ({sig(L1.scores.model.auc)}
        against {sig(P1.auc)}), and the skill is <b>not</b> a spatial artefact: refitting so that
        no test cell was ever seen in training costs only <b>{S1.delta_auc.toFixed(4)}</b> AUC at
        one day and <b>{S7.delta_auc.toFixed(4)}</b> at seven. But AUC is the wrong instrument for
        a {pct(L1.base_rate, 2)} event. Average precision is <b>{sig(L1.scores.model.ap)}</b>, and
        at an operational alert budget the model is right {pct(L1.scores.model.p1, 0)} of the time
        at one day and <b>{pct(L7.scores.model.p1, 0)}</b> at seven, catching
        {pct(L7.scores.model.r1, 1)} of the season's fires. AUC and average precision <b>disagree
        about which baseline is stronger</b>. The FWI comparison was thinner than it looked: the
        reviewed build scored it on <b>one held-out season, {nf(RB.published_n)} cell-days</b>,
        because three CEMS seasons — including the {RB.anchor_years_without_fwi.join(", ")} anchor —
        had been stranded by a defect in the case's own Copernicus queue driver. We diagnosed and
        fixed it, the record drained to {A.fwi.years_on_disk.length} of 15 years, and the
        comparison now runs on <b>all {A.fwi.fold_years_with_fwi.length} held-out seasons at a
        {pct(FW1.join_share, 0)} join</b>. The claim survives but had been flattered: the model's
        recomputed Brier skill against the index at one day falls from
        +{sig(RB.bss_vs_fwi["1"], 3)} to <b>+{sig(P1.fwi_bss, 3)}</b>. The index's own value,
        {sig(FW1.like_for_like.fwi_auc)}, sits inside the
        {LIT.mortelmans.fwi_drained}–{LIT.mortelmans.fwi_undrained} band
        <a href="#ref-mortelmans">Mortelmans et al. (2025)</a> publish for the same box — but the
        composite FWI is <b>not the best member of its own family</b> here: the Build-Up Index
        beats it at every lead, reproducing both the 2007 design decision behind the operational
        Indonesian fire-danger system and the 2025 literature. On transport, the ensemble
        half-width reaches <b>{TR.ensemble[6].med.toFixed(0)} km</b> at 72 hours with only
        {pct(TR.ensemble[6].surv, 0)} of parcels still in the domain; the top attributed province
        changes on {pct(1 - TR.truncation.top_province_same_at_24h, 0)} of episodes if the window
        is 24 hours instead of 72; and across {nf(SG.episode_days)} Singapore episode days the
        most frequently attributed upwind province is <b>{SG.provinces[0].p}</b>
        ({pct(SG.provinces[0].share)}), not South Sumatra ({pct(southSumatra.share)}) — a result
        that is partly a proximity artefact of the weighting and partly a reproduction of
        <a href="#ref-hansen">Hansen et al. (2019)</a>, whose tagged dispersion modelling concluded
        that peninsular Malaysia is a large source for Singapore.</p>
        <p><b>Conclusion.</b> This is a <i>ranking</i> instrument for where to put a patrol, not a
        <i>probability</i> instrument for whether a given cell will burn, and not an attribution
        instrument at province level beyond about a day of travel. Read that way it is defensible
        and unusually well built. Read the way the page currently sells it, it promises more than
        the evidence carries.</p>
      </section>

